      "title": "Why this many sets?",
      "topic": "5. Volume: hard sets per session and diminishing returns",
      "oneLiner": "More hard sets generally build more muscle, with each extra set adding less; the per-session set numbers are a practical guide, not a tested limit.",
      "oneLinerSource": "evidence.md One-line answer",
      "summary": "Sets stopped far from failure stimulate less growth, so Lift only counts sets within about 0–3 reps in reserve (RIR) as hard sets. That's a counting convention, not a proven cutoff. More hard sets generally mean more growth, but each extra set adds less than the one before. In an early analysis, doing multiple sets per exercise was linked to about 40% larger growth effects than single sets. Spreading sets across sessions keeps each session shorter and fresher, which is practical advice: growth is similar either way when weekly hard sets are equal.",
      "recommendation": "Do about 2–4 hard sets per exercise and roughly 3–8 hard sets per muscle per session. Add sets only while performance holds and recovery is good. Splitting volume across days is a practical choice, not a growth requirement.",
      "evidenceStrength": "Strong that more hard sets help, with diminishing returns (Pelland 2026, which also counts indirect sets, such as triceps on bench press, as half a set; Schoenfeld 2017; Krieger 2010). Trials in untrained people agree (Radaelli 2015, Hammarström 2020, Kassiano 2024). In trained lifters, raising volume well above what they were already doing did not add growth in two trials (Barsuhn 2025; Camargo 2026, trained adults, vastus lateralis only, +120% vs +20% over habitual volume, similar growth and no impairment), while an individualised increase did in one small trial (Scarpelli 2022); both suggest setting the dose from each lifter's recent volume rather than a fixed number. The 0–3 RIR hard-set cutoff is a counting convention: Robinson 2024 shows a continuous dose-response, not that easier sets count for nothing. Neither cited meta-regression shows that spreading sets across sessions increases growth (Pelland 2026 found frequency effects on hypertrophy compatible with negligible), so that part is practical reasoning. **The per-exercise and per-session numbers (2–4 hard sets per exercise, 3–8 per muscle per session) are a Lift practical heuristic, not a study finding.** No study pins down a per-session ceiling; one meta-regression of 111 studies linked more sets per workout to slightly smaller whole-body gains (Benito 2020, in men), but it compared across studies, used whole-body measures and did not identify a specific ceiling.",
