      "summary": "Short sessions can work if they keep the hard sets: trained men gained strength with just three 13-minute sessions a week, although longer, higher-volume sessions built more muscle. Pairing opposing exercises or using supersets fit more work into less time in short-term studies, and a 2025 meta-analysis found supersets built similar muscle and strength to traditional sets, based on only two long-term studies.",
      "recommendation": "For short sessions, prioritise multi-joint exercises, pair non-competing exercises as supersets or use drop sets on isolation exercises, and keep warm-ups to exercise-specific ramp-up sets; use longer sessions to add sets for priority muscles.",
      "evidenceStrength": "Moderate for strength with minimal time (several trials), weaker for hypertrophy, where more volume tended to build more muscle. Superset and paired-set evidence is mostly acute or short-term; a 2025 meta-analysis (Zhang 2025) found similar long-term hypertrophy with supersets, but long-term hypertrophy trials remain scarce. A meta-analysis of drop sets (Sødal 2023; 6 studies, 142 participants) found similar hypertrophy to traditional sets, with some drop-set formats taking a half to a third of the time.",
