      "oneLiner": "Count a hard set as 1 for the muscle it targets and ½ for helper muscles; more sets help, but each extra set adds a bit less.",
      "oneLinerSource": "evidence.md One-line answer",
      "summary": "More weekly hard sets per muscle generally produce more growth, with smaller gains from each additional set. Compound lifts also train helper muscles, and a 2026 meta-regression of 67 studies fit the data best when those indirect sets counted as half a set. Some muscles get little from compound lifts: squats grew the quadriceps but not the rectus femoris or hamstrings in one trial in untrained men, and leg extensions grew the rectus femoris far more than squats in another in untrained women.",
      "recommendation": "Track weekly hard sets per muscle as direct sets × 1 plus indirect sets × ½, start from the weekly sets you already do and add sets gradually only while performance keeps improving, and add a direct exercise for muscles that compound lifts train poorly (rectus femoris, hamstrings).",
      "evidenceStrength": "Moderate overall. The evidence is strong across meta-analyses that more weekly hard sets produce more growth with diminishing returns; the overall grade is Moderate because the indirect-set weighting and the trials in trained lifters are uncertain. Individual trials in trained people are mixed: Schoenfeld 2019 and Brigatto 2022 found benefits, Aube 2022, Heaselgrave 2019 and Enes 2024 found no significant differences, and a large jump above lifters' usual volume (+120% vs +20%) gave similar growth to a modest one (Camargo 2026). The null trials are small and may reflect a plateau or too little power to detect a difference. In untrained men over 6 months, 5 sets per exercise grew arm muscles more than 3 or 1 (Radaelli 2015). The ½ weighting for indirect sets is a statistical best fit across many studies, not a measured value per muscle; real contributions vary from near zero (rectus femoris and hamstrings in squats) to about half (biceps in rows) to roughly full (biceps in lat pulldowns, one untrained trial). The two single- vs multi-joint biceps trials (Gentil 2015, Mannarino 2021), both in untrained men, disagree on how much indirect work counts; the ½ weighting sits between them. Most data come from young, mostly male participants.",
